upheld built-screen decline 1: the director re-ruled it MINOR, labelling only, once shown the run's request log (validation-1/hosted-notices-results.json): 844x390-light-503-composer-replacement.png starts light, press 1 asks dark (refused), press 2 asks light (refused), its notice replaces press 1's; 1280x844-dark-503-composer-replacement.png starts dark, press 1 light, press 2 dark, likewise. 390x844-light-503-overall-after.png and 768x1024-dark-503-touch-after.png are first-press pictures. No picture contradicts AC-2.
upheld built-screen decline 2: upheld; AC-5 states with no picture in the review set: 360 dark start, 768 and 844 saved, 1280 dark-to-light saved, the phone sheet collapsed after a switch or refusal, the lights control's accessible name and pressed state, spoken screen-reader output. The run's 81 captures are listed in validation-1/capture-manifest.json.
upheld built-screen decline 4: upheld; focus stays on the pressed control while the note, card and reading place are kept.
decision other-task, owner the subspace-web task that owns the shell phone composer layout (findings 3): The subspace-web owner of the shell phone composer layout decides whether lights should be reachable there; the validation report keeps labelling the open-composer cases callback-only.
decision other-task, owner the subspace-web task that owns the review shell controls (findings 5): The subspace-web owner of the shell controls decides on 44px hit areas; this report cites the 44px lights tile and says the others are not shown.
decision other-task, owner the subspace-web task that owns the shell snackbar (findings 6, 8): The subspace-web owner of the snackbar decides whether it should avoid the composer and panel buttons in landscape.
decision other-task, owner the subspace-web task that owns the shell composer (findings 7): The subspace-web owner of the shell composer decides whether the pair should match the card composer.
